\details{
Pearson's \eqn{r} correlates the (transformed) values; Spearman's
\eqn{\rho} is Pearson's \eqn{r} of their ranks (ties given their average
rank); Kendall's \eqn{\tau_b} counts concordant and discordant pairs of
samples, corrected for ties. All three are the values of
\code{\link[stats:cor]{stats::cor()}}.

\strong{Which transformation.} Spearman and Kendall depend only on the order
of each feature's values across samples, so transformations of one
feature at a time (logs, ranks) don't change them. Transformations of one
sample at a time do: relative abundances (\code{norm = 'percent'}, the
default, and what most papers correlate) rank the samples differently
from raw counts. Relative abundances are compositional, so a taxon can
appear to correlate with a variable only because other taxa do (closure;
see \code{\link[=vlr]{vlr()}}); \code{norm = 'clr'} (with \code{pseudocount}) is the safer choice,
with any method. With many zeros, Spearman's \eqn{\rho} on relative
abundances is dominated by presence and absence: every zero gets the same
rank.

\strong{Partial correlations} (\code{given}): both tables' values (their ranks,
for Spearman, and the covariates' ranks too) are residualised on the
covariates (with an intercept) by least squares, and the result is
Pearson's \eqn{r} of the residuals, with \eqn{n - 2 - k} degrees of
freedom for \eqn{k} covariates. This is \code{ppcor::pcor.test()}'s
definition.

\strong{Speed.} The correlations are computed in C, in parallel over the
features of \code{x}, without forming the joint \eqn{(p + q) \times (p + q)}
matrix that \code{\link[stats:cor]{stats::cor()}} of the two tables bound together computes.
Sparse tables stay sparse: the sums run over each feature's non-zero
samples only.
}
\section{P-values}{

With \code{permutations = 0}, Pearson's and Spearman's p-values are
two-sided, from Student's \eqn{t = r \sqrt{(n - 2 - k) / (1 - r^2)}}
with \eqn{n - 2 - k} degrees of freedom (\eqn{k = 0} without \code{given}),
as \code{scipy.stats.pearsonr()} and \code{spearmanr()}, \code{Hmisc::rcorr()} and
\code{psych::corr.test()} compute them. \eqn{|r| = 1} gives \eqn{p = 0}.
Kendall's is the normal approximation with the tie-corrected variance
(Kendall 1970), as \code{scipy.stats.kendalltau(method = 'asymptotic')}.

With \code{permutations}, the rows of \code{y} are permuted, the same permutation
for every pair (which keeps the correlations within each table), and
\eqn{p = (1 + \#\{|r_b| \ge |r|\}) / (B + 1)} for \eqn{B}
permutations. With \code{given}, the residuals of \code{y} are permuted (ter
Braak 1992). With \code{NA}s in \code{y}, the variables with the same samples
missing share their permutations, of their own samples; a
\code{permutations} matrix then can't be used.

The p-values are then adjusted over \strong{all} \eqn{p \times q} pairs at
once (Benjamini & Hochberg 1995 by default). None of these p-values
allows for the correlations between taxa, or between variables, which
make the pairs' tests far from independent.
}

\section{Missing values}{

\code{x} can't contain \code{NA}s. Each pair uses the samples where its \code{y}
variable is not \code{NA} (pairwise complete observations, as
\code{Hmisc::rcorr()} and \code{cor(use = 'pairwise.complete.obs')}), and the
ranks are taken over those samples. Pairs with fewer than
\code{min_samples} samples, and pairs with a feature or variable that is
constant over their samples, get \code{NA} (the latter with a warning, as
\code{\link[stats:cor]{stats::cor()}}).
}

\section{Differences from cor.test(), Hmisc, psych and microeco}{

\itemize{
\item \code{\link[stats:cor.test]{stats::cor.test()}} gives Spearman's p-value from the exact
distribution of \eqn{\rho} (AS 89) for fewer than 1290 samples without
ties, and Kendall's from its exact distribution for fewer than 50
samples without ties; \code{cor.test(exact = FALSE)} gives the
approximations used here (the same values, ties included).
\item \code{Hmisc::rcorr()} correlates the two tables bound together, the whole
\eqn{(p + q) \times (p + q)} matrix, with the same p-values; it
computes them as one minus a probability, so those below about
\eqn{10^{-8}} are accurate only to about \eqn{10^{-16}} absolutely.
It doesn't adjust them.
\item \code{psych::corr.test(x, y, adjust = 'BH')} gives the same values and
p-values, adjusted over the \eqn{p \times q} pairs, as here.
\item microeco's \code{trans_env$cal_cor(method = 'spearman')} uses
\code{\link[stats:cor.test]{stats::cor.test()}}, so its p-values are exact (above) for pairs
without ties; by default (\code{p_adjust_type = 'All'}) it adjusts over
all pairs too, and it can instead adjust within each taxon or each
variable.
\item \code{ppcor::pcor.test()} gives the same partial correlations and
p-values, one pair at a time (ranking the covariates too, for
Spearman).
}
}

\section{Pseudocount}{

The \code{pseudocount} parameter is only relevant when \code{norm = 'clr'}
or \code{norm = 'log'}.

Zeros are undefined in the centered log-ratio (CLR) transformation and
in the log of proportions. If \code{norm} is \code{'clr'} or
\code{'log'}, \code{pseudocount} is \code{NULL} (the default), and
zeros are detected, the function uses half the minimum non-zero value
(\code{min(x[x>0]) / 2}) and issues a warning.

To suppress the warning, provide an explicit value (e.g., \code{1}), or
replace the zeros first with \code{\link{replace_zeros}()}, whose
result needs no pseudocount.

\strong{Why this matters:} The choice of pseudocount is not neutral; it acts as
a weighting factor that can significantly distort downstream results,
especially for sparse datasets. See Gloor et al. (2017) and Kaul et al.
(2017) for open-access discussions on the mathematical implications, or
Costea et al. (2014) for the impact on community clustering.

See \code{\link{aitchison}} for references.
}

\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

\examples{
    # Taxa against three simulated environmental variables
    set.seed(1)
    counts <- matrix(rpois(30 * 8, 20), 30, 8,
      dimnames = list(paste0('S', 1:30), paste0('Taxon', 1:8)))
    env <- data.frame(
      pH   = counts[, 1] / 10 + rnorm(30),
      temp = rnorm(30, 15),
      salt = c(rnorm(28), NA, NA),
      row.names = rownames(counts) )

    res <- cross_assoc(counts, env)
    res

    # The p x q matrix of correlations
    round(as.matrix(res), 2)

    plot(res)

}
